WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.000 align:start position:0%
 
Kenneth Claire has spent more than three  decades in prison for the rape and  murder of a young woman in Orange County  California but he's been seeking a  retrial  ever since private eye CJ Ford uncovered  the fact that the County DA had tested  DNA evidence found on the scene and that  it didn't match Claire the District  Attorney office really had it in their  minds that we have the right person and  we're gonna prosecute him and we're  gonna put him on death row and you're  gonna see that he's executed question  question question about what they are  not doing for Kenneth and why they are  holding things that could help him  the Orange County DA withheld the DNA  results from the defense at the time of  Claire's earlier Appeals but this isn't  the only piece of information the county  withheld in this case and prosecutors  withholding favorable information isn't  a problem limited to the Clair case or  to Orange County when it comes to  deciding what evidence a jury should  hear when deciding innocence or guilt  the American criminal justice system  entrusts prosecutors with extraordinary  power the issues in the Clair case  certainly the one that's been getting  the most attention is the DNA results  and the fact that they haven't been  turned over but then in an overall sense  the question is what else may be being  held back Scott Sanders is an assistant  public defender in Orange County he's  made a name for himself by exposing the  fact that county prosecutors recruit  jailhouse informants to solicit  confessions from their fellow inmates in  exchange for reduced sentences and then  testify in court and it's done all  without revealing the nature of the  payoff to the jury the key issue with  incentives is playing by the rules I  mean if you're going to give an  incentive be upfront about it disclose  it to the defense and let jurors and  judges know what's going on that's the  only way you're gonna get a fair trial  if you play games with that you risks  very much the prospect of people being  wrongfully incarcerated jailhouse  informants don't play a role in the  Clair case but Sanders believes that  there are serious problems with at least  two witnesses in the case  the first is Pauline Flores Claire's  ex-girlfriend who supposedly caught  Clair confessing  on tape that's not a confession just  like hey just like leave me alone type  of thing regardless of the credibility  issues with this alleged confession one  big problem is that the prosecutor and  the witness denied to the jury that she  received anything in return for her  cooperation it was only decades later  that she admitted that the police took  care of an outstanding warrant for  wearing a wire and extracting something  that sounded like a confession the  reason why it's important to disclose  information regarding a witness in any  context is jurors are making an  assessment about credibility so if a  witness comes in and says I'm here out  of the goodness of my heart or I'm here  just because I'm interested in helping  the justice system when in fact there's  a benefit one of the issues is an  important issue is should that jury find  the witness credible or not that  credibility determination especially for  a witness like Pauline for Flores is  critical another witness was Margaret  hustling the owner of the house where  the murder occurred who testified that  Claire stole a turquoise charm  belongings to the victim on the night of  the crime Sanders discovered that  hustling was in the middle of a big  welfare fraud case at the time of  Claire's trial prosecutors in that case  downgraded her crime from a felony to a  misdemeanor and a $500 fine after she  provided special info on another case  only 13 days after Claire was convicted  and sentenced to death  jury in the capital case never sees that  she gets this enormous benefit it's bad  enough because it's kind of obvious on  its face but we've seen this pattern in  multiple cases where as soon as the jury  comes to their verdict in the case that  the prosecution's handling they go out  and give a pretty extraordinary  resolution in the witness's case the  prosecutor in the case was a man named  Michael Jacobs he ran Orange County's  Homicide Unit four years and obtained  convictions in several death penalty  cases Michael Jacobs to me and I was  gonna tell you personally very evil  prosecutor he was recognition he likes  the idea of win at all costs were  looking at a prosecutor who on  number of cases did not have discovery  turned over on a number of capital cases  the worry here is what else is out there  that hasn't been turned over Michael  Jacobs won a conviction against a man  named William Charles Payton in 1980 for  a rape and murder after a jailhouse  informant helped secure a death penalty  conviction for the prosecution and  exchange for early release the jury  never knew about the deal and the  informant went on to commit a murder on  the outside California executed Thomas  Thompson in 1998 for a rape and murder  once again the testimony of jailhouse  informants sealed his fate and once  again the jury in the defense never  heard about the deals they cut with the  county the prosecutor in the case was  Michael Jacobs we know he's handled a  lot of homicides he was in the Homicide  Unit for a number of years he was the  head of the homicide unit do we just  assume that somehow in his other cases  he turned over favorable evidence we  should just go on and those people in  custody should die the death that no one  wants which is a death in a prison after  spending many years or should they just  die without having a Rhian in the case I  don't think so I think every one of his  cases should be re-examined Jacobs  declined multiple requests to  participate in this documentary but  Sanders discovered old correspondence  between Jacobs and the state attorney  general's office handling a number of  Appeals in his death penalty cases in  one letter Jacobs complains that the  office finds it necessary to show  everything we have to the defense saying  this is a new concept for me the DA  later fired Jacobs but in its  termination letter didn't cite anything  about his attitude towards withholding  evidence and seemed most upset about his  insulting and demeaning tone when  interacting with the state attorney  general's office what they were learning  in those letters should have been enough  to open investigation about every one of  Michael Jacobs cases once you pass this  line where we lose faith and whether  you're turning over evidence and you're  always turning it over and you're making  that assessment and you're worrying  deeply about defendants rights a case  like clear where there's already  problems boy we should all be worried  but just how widespread is the problem  of withholding evidence in the criminal  justice system this problem is not  limited to Orange County or any one  jurisdiction this is a problem that  we've seen across the whole country you  can see it from our data we found that  there was an inordinate number of cases  involving exactly this problem Cathleen  cookie Ridolfi is a law professor at  Santa Clara University and co-author of  a study on evidence disclosure when you  look at the problems with what  prosecutors do we're always looking at  the prosecutors themselves what we  decided to do was not look at the  prosecutors but look at the courts look  at judges look at judicial rulings to  see to the extent to which what the  courts are doing in some way is either  encouraging non-disclosure or  encouraging disclosure Ridolfi and her  colleagues examined about 5,000 cases  and found 620 where prosecutors withheld  favorable evidence judges ruled that the  withheld evidence only constituted a  violation of law in 22 of those cases  courts are just okay with prosecutors  withholding favorable evidence and  that's really troubling because we know  from post-conviction analysis of DNA  cases that the withholding of favorable  evidence is a key cause of wrongful  conviction another trend of the  researchers uncovered was that  prosecutors were most likely to withhold  evidence in death penalty cases the  truth is actually that finding is  consistent with what I've been finding  in all of our studies which is that you  tend to see more problems in the more  serious cases and I have to I can  speculate and say that the fact that  prosecutors have so much at stake in a  serious case particularly when it's a  death penalty case to win the case their  motivation may be a little greater to  across the line and it's not only that  the cheating gets worse as the stakes  get higher but it appears that the  prosecutors who cheat once tend to do it  again and again Ridolfi found in  california that 1/3 of prosecutorial  misconduct cases were against  prosecutors whom courts had found guilty  of misconduct before without facing  discipline there are primary prosecutors  out there who are doing the right thing  what I say to them is it's important  that we stand together because those  prosecutors who are deviant are actually  they're cutting into the integrity of  the whole system when you  primary objective is to win convictions  it explains all the problems that we're  talking about because the prosecutors  job is to act in the interest of justice  and come up with and reach the right  result not to win convictions if you  want to wait convictions you take  shortcuts you hide evidence you don't  disclose deals you've got to put winning  way secondary to everything else if you  do that if you say look what we want you  to do number one is disclose what you're  supposed to and will live with bet with  bad outcomes will live with acquittals  when they come then the system's gonna  work a lot more fairly one of the  reasons for real concern on a case like  clear is we now identified some issues  for concern and they're pretty  substantial but the real concern  probably more so is what else wasn't  turned over what do you do in those  cases with the failure to disclose that  in the case like clear the right outcome  at the very minimum is to give him a new  trial I couldn't believe that the DA was  sitting on all this evidence now I'm not  testing so I found out that they were  testing it and they knew that the DNA  didn't match Claire's they didn't find  any  Claire's fingerprints they found someone  else's fingerprints but there's nothing  on the crime scene that belongs to  Claire

